{
  "requirement": "`habeas setup` MUST preserve existing entries in `output.html.additional-css` while adding the Habeas stylesheet at most once. It MUST report an error if `additional-css` is not an array.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** `requirements.cli.setup_css` is implemented completely and tested. I found no unmarked implementation and no unspecified behaviour.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `fn run` in `src/setup.rs` (around lines 125–143, marked `{impl}`) handles the three cases for `output.html.additional-css`:\n  - **Absent:** it creates an array containing the Habeas stylesheet path.\n  - **An array:** it pushes the stylesheet path only if no existing entry already equals it. Existing entries are left untouched, so they are preserved and the stylesheet appears at most once.\n  - **Anything else:** it returns the error `output.html.additional-css must be an array`.\n- `css_relative` (marked `{impl}`) computes the stylesheet path from the book source directory as `<src>/habeas.css`, using only normal path components with `/` separators.\n- `is_configured` in `src/setup.rs` only `{cite}`s this requirement. It is the read-only check for the Symposium setup hook (`requirements.integration.symposium_setup_hook`) and does not write the CSS configuration. Citing it rather than marking it `{impl}` is therefore appropriate.\n\n**Tests**\n- `setup_is_repeatable` in `tests/setup.rs` starts from a fixture whose `book.toml` already has `additional-css = [\"md/custom.css\"]`. It runs `setup` twice and asserts three things:\n  - `md/custom.css` is preserved.\n  - `md/habeas.css` is present.\n  - `md/habeas.css` occurs exactly once after the repeat run.\n- `setup_rejects_invalid_book_configuration` in `tests/setup.rs` sets `additional-css = \"wrong\"` and asserts that `setup` fails with `output.html.additional-css must be an array`.\n\n**Residual gaps (minor, not blocking)**\n- No test covers the case where `additional-css` is absent. The code path is simple.\n- No test covers a pre-existing Habeas entry on the first run. The idempotence check on the second run exercises the same check.",
